{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-168840",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y-1}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=0$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=2.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134723/assets/4f9c46e1fa6cc373896c2905aab4c1ceca6ff4de7cd498297d4a02e5c4375a39.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=0$ and leaves through the upper function $x={e}^{y-1}.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le {e}^{y-1}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {e}^{y-1}\\}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
